{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1551238-playing-around-with-concepts-or-trying-to-fit-together-that-which-doesn-t","id":1551238,"title":"Playing around with concepts, or: Trying to fit together that which doesn't","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-05-23T13:01:24-07:00","id":1551238,"title":"Playing around with concepts, or: Trying to fit together that which doesn't","content":"Sup, I'm back.<br /> So, what's this all about?<br /> <br /> Perhaps unsurprisingly, I like the Xianxia/Cultivation genre. I like the ChroD. I figured there must be a way to mix the two. So I went ahead and started playing around with fluff and mechanics for a hypothetical &quot;Cultivation: The Failing&quot;, fansplat-style.<br /> Yes, I know there are lots of fangames, there is no need for more, and this is essentially fitting a square in a hole (I know Exalted exists; I want something somewhat different). This is mostly just a concept to play around with, but I really wanted to see if one can have a 'Chinese Style Immortals' game with a personal horror theme like the other splat books. Plus if I have a thought or idea like this stuck in my head, it's like a loose tooth - I can't leave it alone until it's out.<br /> The purpose of this thread is twofold: 1. have you played around with ideas that don't really fit the style of ChroD, but you'd have fun playing anyway? What did you do with them?<br /> 2. Feedback, especially on the fluff side of things (mechanics can alway be worked out later somehow, though pointing out things I missed or make no sense are welcome). Do you feel this has potential for Horror-themed games for those of us who also have an old-style Kung Fu movie itch? Why/not?<br /> <br /> So, what would this look like? Cultivators are essentially people using a mystic energy called Qi to prolong their life and gaining nifty powers in the process. Think (pre-shippuden-)Naruto-style ninjas, flying swordsmen, Kung Fu Panda, Kung Fu Hustle, but also Ong Bak (especially 2 and 3), Drunken Master movies, Hero, Crouching Tiger Hidden Dragon, 36th Chamber of Shaolin, Big Trouble in Little China among others. Fancy, flowery names, obtuse mythology references, &quot;Omoe wa mo shindeiru&quot; shenanigans etc included.<br /> Their X-Axis (Body, Soul, Elemental, Bloodline, Artifact, Demonic) would be based on their main Cultivation Style, and due to that include the axis-specific benefits like passive bonuses (physical resistance bonus, perception bonus, environmental tilt resistance, along those lines etc) as well as their in-Style Techniques and Arts. Y-Axis would be whether they join one of the few remaining sects warring for resources, or remain alone (less obligations and more freedom, but also fewer resources and guidance). Qi is gained through Meditation, consuming resources like ambient Qi or special items like alchemical pills, and winning battles. Qi comes in different levels (Normal, Refined, Pure), and a Stage 1 Cultivator cannot use Pure Qi, while a Stage 5 cultivator cannot satisfy his needs with normal Qi. Cycling Qi is an extended meditation and needs to happen regularly (less often and much shorter for low Stages). Higher stages = stronger baseline bonuses, access to more powers, and much higher life expectancy (Stage 1 some 220 Years, Stage 2 340 , Stage 3 560 , Stage 4 1100 , Stage 5 3400, Stage 10 would be simply truly Immortal).<br /> To find the rare ingredients they need for their alchemical pills and elixiers, they need to traverse the world, find almost untouched places, and delve into secret pocket realms that riddle our world like a multi-Matroshka doll. Antagonists are all those other pesky cultivators who don't want to share their resources and use up yours, even though that reduces your chances of becoming the best/living forever/... For some reason, Stage 5+ cultivators seems to disappear, apparently going off into other worlds.<br /> <br /> So far, so normal for a fantasy game. How do we add Horror? My first thought was that running out of Qi should be worse than just &quot;Oh, I ran out of gas!&quot;, and so we can employ the nifty concepts of Qi Deviation and Heart Demons (tropes of the genre I feel are underutilized). Qi Deviation can range from long-term conditions that are hard to resolve to rages that invert one's character (so during this time, it's not 'mindless rage', but rather the player character essentially turns into your worst possible enemy who wants to see you suffer as much as possible for a short duration, causing the most emotional, mental and physical harm possible). The longer one goes without circulating Qi, the worse the effects (time progression decreases with increasing power level, e.g. Stage 1 has a week before the stage progresses, Stage 5 has an hour). Getting rid of these might require retreating into Seclusion - finding a natural nice place with lots of Qi, sit down, don't move for a long while and focus on circulating your Qi, chanting obscure incomprehensible scriptures and after a really long time come back. Good luck explaining to your boss why you need a 6 months vacation! (The higher the Stage, the longer the seclusion - Stage 5 might have to retreat for decades or even centuries!)<br /> Power Stat does not increase with xp, but rather Insight - essentially, kinda like in Promethean, where making certain experiences helps them understand how to be human, certain experiences help cultivators understand the Dao. Collecting enough Insight results in a chance to break through the next stage. Breaking through is a very sensitive and defenseless moment, where even a child could cause catastrophic damage to you by brushing your shoulder. Two dangers await here: Heart Demons and Heavenly Tribulations. Heart Demons take many shapes and come to be due to 'lacking' preperation: you let the murderer of your parents get away in the last fight? That'll weigh on your conscience and become a problem. You try to advance with the help of ingredients you stole form your friends? Same. You strive to far from the beaten path of cultivation, using contradicting elements or similar things? There's a risk a heart demon develops and stops you from advancing, instead causing you Lethal Damage. But there's more! It can stick around if you're unlucky, causing you hallucinations, restricted access to your techniques, reduced Qi pool, lack of Willpower (unable to spend or loss of a Dot), repeated nighmares (think a Starving Beast's Horror visits you repeatedly for a snack) even physical mutations. Resolving it can be a questline unto itself. Resolvin a heart demon not only undoes the restrictions, but comes with a sizeable bonus on the next Breakthrough attempt. You can acquire more than one of these, of course.<br /> Regarding fighting: take Mage's obsession for the supernatural, and transpose it on martial arts and fighting. They NEED to fight to gain Insights (it's not the only way, but it's a mandatory one), but losing a fight also means running out of Qi (unless the opponent is, for some reason, really kind). They do not need to fight cultivators all the time, although at some point, they won't be able to not seek out cultivators or risk no longer progressing or even regressing to a lower stage (if Fate does not provide cultivators to cross their paths - usually at inopportune moments).<br /> <br /> Regarding gaining Qi, you can do the above methods, or you can rip it straight from living beings, with immediate, permanent harmful results like permanent conditions, being Wasted (all the drawbacks of a cultivator with NONE of the benefits) and being turned into a demonic human - and you risk becoming addicted to the rush of it (so there is no 'harmless' Qi Absorption). This does offer much more Qi much more quickly, however. Same as turning humans into Qi pills through alchemy, which you can then nom on in your leisure time - they are guaranteed to succeed, unlike almost any other type of pill. You can also rip Qi out of the environment with a somewhat lengthy ritual, but you can do so anywhere! Only problem is, it leaves that area devoid of Qi forever...<br /> And places without Qi become strange before they inevitably become incapable of spiritually supporting life (in cross-overs, Uratha and Mages would find these places creepy, because they are essentially disconnected from any other plane of existence like the Supernal Realms, and also slowly becoming lifeless deserts).<br /> Why do they not mess with humans despite their power level being over 9000? For one, numbers and modern weaponry (especially artillery). Even a Stage 5 Cultivator can't do much about being hit with an A-Bomb. So they try not get awake the people's ire. In addition, the aforementioned Heavenly Tribulations. See, when too many people see cultivators use their nifty skills, the Will of the Heavens, does, too. And it does not like these people going against the natural order one bit, so they are faced with Divine Lightning, Samsara Flames or similar catastrophes if they pull out all stops too often. If they try to keep if on the down-low, but mess up a few times in the same general area, the tools are less obvious, but just as effective: the threads of Fate move just enough to ensure you are ambushed by police during a breakthrough, or cross paths with a group of demonic or simply antagonistic cultivators. Or perhaps you getting that last Withered Dragon Bone Flower for your breakthrough pills meant someone else's granddaughter couldn't get their antidote and died, and now a Sect Elder wants to see you dead. Or maybe you just crossed a Dragon's Nest, and now a group of Kindred from the Ordo Dracul take an unhealthy amount of interest in you....<br /> Why the Will of the Heavens picks on them, or if these actions are somehow necessary, no body knows. But people being what they are, theories abound.<br /> <br /> So, backlore: most Cultivators believe that at some point, the world was filled with pure Dao essence, and the Will of the Heavens supported them. Cultivators were powerful, and traversed an endless chain of worlds, each bigger and more beautiful, with stronger spirit beasts, purer Qi and better materials than the last. So of course, someone messed with it, believing the Will of the Heavens was flawed and limited them. They tried to supplant it, a war broke out that left most of existence in ashes, the remainder of the worlds at war with each other, and the Will of the Heavens broken and antagonistic. The Chain of Worlds was disjointed and broken, with many worlds burning each other down.<br /> The place we life in? A backwater so unimportant it essentially only still exists because everyone ignored it during the War of the Heavens. But if you ask whether there are still other worlds, whether you can still connect to them, and whether or not the War is even over or still rages, you'll get eleven opinions from ten people.<br /> <br /> The worst part of this broken Chain of Worlds, though? Qi seems to run out. If not circulated by Cultivators, places diminish their levels of Qi little by little, leaving empty deserts. Places devoid of Qi are lost except for the most dramatic actions (sacrificing one of a major sects' Grand Treasures, for example). But as long as there is still even a little Qi, they can be repaired. If all Cultivators cooperated, they could repair major parts of the world and might even be able to restore it in the long term. That would help them out a lot, because if you have enough places with Qi, you can tap a little from each to get enough, and if you set them up well, the dense Qi is enough for several cultivators. But ironically, the short-sightedness of these long-lived warriors of will and power leads to them sacrificing whole patches of places to gain a few more centuries. In addition, even though sect's maintain areas of dense Qi , one of the most effective means in a Sect War is Devouring that very spot... so some worry that our world is destined to end up yet another dead spot in the ruined Chain of Worlds.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So yeah, help with identifying, isolating and cleaning up the themes would be appreciated. Also, your thoughts on how the other splats would view these weirdos that always want to punch one another?<br /> <br /> <br /> (P.S.: I was not aware of <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1505310-for-fun-splats-as-spiritual-xianxia-cultivation-methods-scriptures-styles\">this thread</a> at the time of writing this -.-)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8542,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","user":"Wormwood","date":". 05-23-2025, 04:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-10T15:02:03-07:00","id":1551571,"title":"","content":"This already exists, canonically, and have been for quite some time:<br /> <br /> Second Sight, the tradition of Taoist Alchemist.<br /> <br /> It is <b>literally</b> the rules based on the actual traditions of cultivators.<br /> <br /> Cultivators are a real thing, with real practices and beliefs, related to Taoism and to Qi/Chi, (which isn't a strange energy, is life energy that circulates in the body with breathing. Everyone has Chi).<br /> <br /> My suggestion: while SS's Tao Alchemist isn't full Wuxia style, it's a good enough starting point. Make adjustments and new Merits for the effects you want to add, borrow a few effects from major splats (specially the Physical Discipline from Vampire), observe if something you want is better served as a power or as a house-rule for the system itself. In some cases, it may be that certain Immortals are better represented by a splat taking in Taoist Alchemist (a possibility now).<br /> <br /> Wuxia is a genre, not a power set. Making sure the system accommodates the tropes of the genre is more important than trying to translate them into a splat. That'll only make for a Wuxia-like character in a world that doesn't follow. Likewise, Immortals are a diverse bunch that are more a common source for a variety of legends/characters instead of a unified splat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}